{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-37341",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g({e}^{2},1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-{1}^{2}+1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-3\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2}\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+{e}^{-2},-1-2{e}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
